.nh
.TH "CITADEL" "1" "Aug 2026" "AceTeam" "Citadel CLI Manual"

.SH NAME
citadel-up - Put this whole machine on the AceTeam Network (needs sudo)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcitadel up [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Brings up a network interface so EVERY program on this machine can reach
your other nodes directly — ssh, curl, a browser, a database client — using
their network IPs and names.

.PP
This is different from 'citadel login', which connects only the citadel
process itself. Both use the same node identity, so this machine appears once
on your network either way.

.PP
Because it creates a network interface and edits the system routing table,
this command needs administrator privileges. 'citadel login' does not, and
remains the right choice if you only need citadel itself to reach the network.

.PP
Runs in the foreground until interrupted; Ctrl-C takes the machine back off
the network and restores routing and DNS.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--authkey\fP=""
	Pre-generated authkey for non-interactive join

.PP
\fB--check\fP[=false]
	Report whether this machine can run machine-wide mode, then exit (changes nothing)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for up

.PP
\fB--node-name\fP=""
	Override the node name (defaults to hostname)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Enable debug output

.PP
\fB--no-auto-update\fP[=false]
	Disable automatic update checks and installs for this run (or set CITADEL_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	Disable colorized output

.PP
\fB--runtime\fP=""
	Container runtime for module containers: docker or podman (default: auto-detect)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Put this machine on the network (foreground)
  sudo citadel up

  # First-time join with an authkey
  sudo citadel up --authkey tskey-auth-xxx

  # Check whether this machine can do it, without changing anything
  sudo citadel up --check

  # Take the machine back off the network
  sudo citadel down
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcitadel(1)\fP
